ules per gram degree  Kelvin for air the specific heat of air  is one the specific heat of olive oil  something which is also useful for  cooking is about one point nine seven  joules per gram per degree Kelvin so  it's actually a lower specific heat by  about a factor of two than that of water  now actually those two numbers if you  think about them the specific heat of  water and the specific heat of olive oil  the fact that most foods are between  those two numbers is not an accident by  any means I mean in fact the main  ingredient of all food is water this is  something that we're going to have to  think about constantly throughout this  course it will come up again and again  in one context or another and another  important ingredient to food of course  is fat which olive oil is a fat and fats  tend to have a specific heat around too  and food you know like beef it's  somewhere between two to four and that's  essentially why it's a little more  complicated than that but that's not a  bad explanation to get us started now so  to get us started let's actually do  something let's actually do something so  I want to calculate the energy that it  takes to boil one cup of water so it  turns out that one cup of water is 237  grams and so what I want to do is start  with water that's at room temperature so  the temperature in this room is about 23  degrees Celsius and I'd like to heat it  to the boiling point which is a hundred  degrees Celsius and I'd like to know how  much energy do I have to dump in and  when you multiply this out it turns out  that the number that you get is 76  thousand two hundred and eighty joules  so that's how much energy it takes to  boil a cup of water 76210 umber in  joules so one way to think about it how  much would would it take to make this  much energy you know if you take the  energy density of wood which I already  told you is 14 mega joules per kilogram  then you can calculate for yourself that  the that the amount of wood that it 